WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.700
Recently, we have experienced an abundance
of papers on facial reenactment in machine

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.700
learning research.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:18.800
We talked about a technique by the name Face2Face
back in 2016, approximately 300 videos ago.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.670
It was able to take a video of us and transfer
our gestures to a target subject.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:30.659
This was kind of possible at the time with
specialized depth cameras, until Face2Face

00:00:30.659 --> 00:00:35.890
appeared and took the world by storm as it
was able to perform what you see here with

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.020
a regular consumer camera.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:44.190
However, it only transferred gestures, so
of course, scientists were quite excited about

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.000
the possibility of transferring more than
just that.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.429
But, that would require solving so many more
problems - for instance, if we wish to turn

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:58.429
the head of the target subject, we may need
to visualize regions that we haven’t seen

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:04.660
in these videos, which also requires an intuitive
understanding of hair, the human face and

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.760
more.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.680
This is quite challenging.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.350
So, can this be really done?

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.920
Well, have a look at this amazing new paper!

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.690
You see here the left image, this is the source
person, the video on the right is the target

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:27.890
video, and our task is to transfer not just
the gestures, but the pose, gestures and appearance

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.760
of the face on the left to the video on the
right.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.590
And, this new method works like magic.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.700
Look!

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.500
It not only works like magic, but pulls it
off on a surprisingly large variety of cases,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.670
many of which I haven’t expected at all.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.780
Now, hold on to your papers, because this
technique was not trained on these subjects,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.540
which means that this is the first time it
is seeing these people.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.860
It has been trained on plenty of people, but
not these people.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.130
Now, before we look at this example, you are
probably saying, well, the occlusions from

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.690
the microphone will surely throw the algorithm
off, right?

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.430
Well, let’s have a look.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.150
Nope, no issues at all.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.970
Absolutely amazing, love it!

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.720
So how does this wizardry work exactly?

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.290
Well, it requires careful coordination between
no less than four neural networks, where each

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.770
of which specializes for a different task.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:36.500
The first two is a reenactment generator that
produces a first estimation of the reenacted

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:42.270
face, and a segmentation generator network
that creates this colorful image that shows

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.590
which region in the image corresponds to which
facial landmark.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:51.670
These two are then handed over to the third
network, the inpainting generator, which fills

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:57.470
the rest of the image, and since we have overlapping
information, in comes the fourth, blending

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:03.580
generator to the rescue to combine all this
information into our final image.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.570
The paper contains a detailed description
of each of these networks, so make sure to

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.700
have a look!

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.620
And if you do, you will also find that there
are plenty of comparisons against previous

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.840
works, of course, Face2Face is one of them,
which was already amazing, and you can see

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.350
how far we’ve come in only three years.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:28.599
Now, when we try to evaluate such a research
work, we are curious as to how much these

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:34.130
individual puzzle pieces, in this case, the
generator networks contribute to the final

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.260
results.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.590
Are really all of them needed?

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.300
What if we remove some of them?

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.590
Well, this is a good paper, so we can find
the answer in Table 2, where all of these

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.570
components are tested in isolation.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:54.110
The downward and upward arrows show which
measure is subject to minimization and maximization,

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:59.420
and if we look at this column, it is quite
clear that all of them indeed improve the

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.099
situation, and contribute to the final results.

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:07.410
And remember, all this from just one image
of the source person.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.560
Insanity.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.010
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

